saveOutputs: false
cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "# 11a · Step 1 – What do we know about Jonas? (JSON)\n\nMonday, 16 November 2026, 11:17. Jonas sits on a rock above Pottenstein, his right knee is swelling, and tomorrow he gives his first DOAG talk. Before anyone can judge his symptoms, we need his context: the health record from his GP practice and the report he has just sent from the health app. Both arrive as JSON documents.\n\n**Question:** What do we know about Jonas before we look at his knee?\n\n- **Features:** native `JSON` data type, simple dot notation, `JSON_TABLE`, JSON path filters, `JSON_EXISTS`, JSON Schema, JSON Relational Duality View\n- **Pattern:** Foundation for all patterns: documents are collected and read with SQL\n- **Data:** `STG_JSON_DOCS` (all JSON documents of `data/json`, one row per document), relational tables of the care network\n- **Run:** as the demo user (DOAG2026) in SQL Developer for VS Code, after `01_run_all.sql`. Run the cells from top to bottom.\n\n> Fictitious demo: no medical device and no medical advice. Places from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL); all persons and story places are synthetic."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Session settings\n\nThe story takes place on Monday, 16 November 2026, in Franconia. With the session time zone `Europe/Berlin`, all timestamps and literals read in local time.\n\nThe SQLcl settings in the second cell make the text output readable: columns as wide as their content instead of their declared length (`SQLFORMAT ansiconsole`; values from JSON are `VARCHAR2(4000)`), one header per result (`PAGESIZE`), wider lines (`LINESIZE`) and complete JSON documents and CLOBs (`LONG`, `LONGCHUNKSIZE`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "ALTER SESSION SET TIME_ZONE = 'Europe/Berlin';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SET SQLFORMAT ansiconsole\nSET PAGESIZE 1000\nSET LINESIZE 250\nSET LONG 1000000\nSET LONGCHUNKSIZE 250"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 1. Documents in the database\n\nEverything the demo needs arrives as JSON: health records, the app report, the map data. The documents are stored as they come, in the native `JSON` data type.\n\n**Question:** Which documents do we have, and how many of each kind?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The load script `03_load_data.sql` keeps every JSON document in `STG_JSON_DOCS`, column `JSON_DOCUMENT` of the native data type `JSON` (binary format OSON). One row per document:\n\n- `fhir_bundles_all_patients`: one HL7 FHIR bundle (health record) per person, 1,000 bundles\n- `symptom_report_jonas`: Jonas' symptom report from the health app\n- the GeoJSON layers: one document per feature\n\nThe query counts the documents per source (`LAYER`) and the size of their text form in KB.\n\n**Result:** 16,550 documents in 12 layers: 1,000 FHIR bundles, Jonas' app report and the map data. Each GeoJSON layer has one row more than it has features: `DOC_NO` 0 is the header of the FeatureCollection. So the map holds the story places (18 trail segments of the loop, 5 car parks, 3 points of interest, 5 zones) and the OpenStreetMap extracts (2 city boundaries, 37 hospitals, 1,257 paths and hiking routes, 14,078 road segments)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT layer,\n       COUNT(*)                                                                   AS documents,\n       ROUND(SUM(DBMS_LOB.GETLENGTH(JSON_SERIALIZE(json_document RETURNING CLOB))) / 1024) AS text_kb\nFROM   stg_json_docs\nGROUP  BY layer\nORDER  BY layer;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 2. Jonas' health record as a FHIR bundle\n\nJonas' GP practice keeps his record as an HL7 FHIR bundle: one document with all his resources.\n\n**Question:** What does Jonas' complete health record look like?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The simple dot notation navigates a JSON document like an object: `s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".id` is the ID of the first resource in the bundle, the patient. The item method `.string()` returns the value as `VARCHAR2`, so it can be compared in `WHERE`.\n\n`RESOURCE` is a reserved word in Oracle SQL, so the field name must be written in double quotes: `\"resource\"`. Field names in the dot notation are case-sensitive, quoted or not.\n\n`JSON_SERIALIZE … PRETTY` returns the whole document in readable form. It returns `VARCHAR2(32767)` (possible with `MAX_STRING_SIZE = EXTENDED`), because the notebook output shows only the first 80 characters of a CLOB.\n\n**Result:** the bundle `bundle-P0001` with the resources Patient (Jonas), two Conditions (the sprain of his right knee in 2021, hay fever), four Observations (sports profile, height, weight, resting pulse) and a MedicationStatement."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Display only: wrap the document at 200 characters instead of padding it to 32,767\nCOLUMN fhir_bundle FORMAT A200"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT JSON_SERIALIZE(s.json_document RETURNING VARCHAR2(32767) PRETTY) AS fhir_bundle\nFROM   stg_json_docs s\nWHERE  s.layer = 'fhir_bundles_all_patients'\nAND    s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".id.string() = 'P0001';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 3. Master data with the dot notation\n\nFirst the basics: who is Jonas, where does he live, and who is his GP?\n\n**Question:** Which personal data does the record hold?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Item methods convert JSON values into SQL data types: `.string()` to `VARCHAR2`, `.date()` to `DATE`. Arrays are addressed by position (`name[0]`, `given[0]`). The reserved words `resource` and `timestamp` are quoted.\n\n**Result:** Jonas, male, born 1988, lives in Nuremberg, his general practitioner is the organization `PR01`. This ID links the document with the care network (step 5)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".id.string()                              AS patient_id,\n       s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".name[0].given[0].string()                AS first_name,\n       s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".name[0].family.string()                  AS family_name,\n       s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".gender.string()                          AS gender,\n       s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".birthDate.date()                         AS birth_date,\n       s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".address[0].city.string()                 AS city,\n       s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".generalPractitioner[0].reference.string() AS general_practitioner,\n       s.json_document.\"timestamp\".string()                                         AS record_as_of\nFROM   stg_json_docs s\nWHERE  s.layer = 'fhir_bundles_all_patients'\nAND    s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".id.string() = 'P0001';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 4. All resources of the record as rows (`JSON_TABLE`)\n\nA twisted knee may not be his first one. His history can change how today's symptoms are read.\n\n**Question:** Which conditions, observations and medications are in Jonas' record?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`JSON_TABLE` turns the array `entry[*]` into one row per resource: conditions, observations and medication. Each `PATH` reads one value; fields that a resource type does not have stay `NULL`.\n\nThe comma before `JSON_TABLE` is a lateral join: each document is only combined with the rows that come from itself, not with other documents.\n\n**Result:** In 2021 Jonas sprained his right knee (*Distorsion rechtes Knie*, resolved). He hikes two to three times a week. So a twisted knee looks familiar to him – which is exactly why \"just a sprain?\" is the tempting answer."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT jt.resource_type,\n       jt.resource_id,\n       COALESCE(jt.code_text, jt.medication)                            AS description,\n       jt.clinical_status,\n       COALESCE(jt.onset, jt.effective)                                 AS since,\n       COALESCE(jt.value_string, jt.value_number || ' ' || jt.value_unit) AS value\nFROM   stg_json_docs s,\n       JSON_TABLE(s.json_document, '$.entry[*].resource'\n         COLUMNS (resource_type    VARCHAR2(30)  PATH '$.resourceType',\n                  resource_id      VARCHAR2(30)  PATH '$.id',\n                  code_text        VARCHAR2(100) PATH '$.code.text',\n                  clinical_status  VARCHAR2(20)  PATH '$.clinicalStatus.coding[0].code',\n                  onset            VARCHAR2(30)  PATH '$.onsetDateTime',\n                  effective        VARCHAR2(30)  PATH '$.effectiveDateTime',\n                  value_string     VARCHAR2(200) PATH '$.valueString',\n                  value_number     NUMBER        PATH '$.valueQuantity.value',\n                  value_unit       VARCHAR2(20)  PATH '$.valueQuantity.unit',\n                  medication       VARCHAR2(200) PATH '$.medication.concept.text')) jt\nWHERE  s.layer = 'fhir_bundles_all_patients'\nAND    s.json_document.entry[0].\"resource\".id.string() = 'P0001'\nORDER  BY jt.resource_type, jt.resource_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 5. JSON path filters: the diagnoses in all 1,000 records\n\nJonas is one of 1,000 people with a record. Their diagnoses are the experience the similarity search in step 3 will draw on.\n\n**Question:** Which diagnoses were made across all records, and how often?\n\n**How the query answers it:** A filter expression in the row path selects only the resources we need: `?(@.resourceType == \"Condition\" && @.category[0].coding[0].code == \"encounter-diagnosis\")` keeps the diagnoses made by a doctor and skips everything else. Then plain SQL aggregates over the documents.\n\n**Result:** Among the knee diagnoses, sprains (`kniedistorsion`, 115) are more frequent than ruptures of the anterior cruciate ligament (`vkb_ruptur`, 80). The similarity search in step 3 reflects this imbalance."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT jt.diagnosis_code,\n       MIN(jt.diagnosis_label)   AS diagnosis_label,\n       COUNT(*)                  AS diagnoses,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT s.doc_no)  AS records\nFROM   stg_json_docs s,\n       JSON_TABLE(s.json_document,\n         '$.entry[*].resource?(@.resourceType == \"Condition\" && @.category[0].coding[0].code == \"encounter-diagnosis\")'\n         COLUMNS (diagnosis_code   VARCHAR2(60)  PATH '$.code.coding[0].code',\n                  diagnosis_label  VARCHAR2(100) PATH '$.code.text')) jt\nWHERE  s.layer = 'fhir_bundles_all_patients'\nGROUP  BY jt.diagnosis_code\nORDER  BY diagnoses DESC\nFETCH  FIRST 12 ROWS ONLY;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 6. `JSON_EXISTS` with a path variable\n\nTwo diagnoses are at stake for Jonas: a harmless sprain or a ruptured cruciate ligament.\n\n**Question:** How many records mention one or the other?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`JSON_EXISTS` tests whether a path matches anywhere in the document, here: does the record contain a condition with a given code? The value is passed with `PASSING … AS \"code\"` and used as `$code` in the path, like a bind variable. The query counts records with a sprain or a cruciate ligament rupture anywhere in the record (diagnosis or medical history).\n\n**Result:** a sprain appears in 189 records, a cruciate ligament rupture in 128. These numbers are higher than in section 5 because they also count earlier conditions in the medical history – like Jonas' sprain from 2021."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT 'kniedistorsion' AS condition_code,\n       COUNT(*)         AS records\nFROM   stg_json_docs s\nWHERE  s.layer = 'fhir_bundles_all_patients'\nAND    JSON_EXISTS(s.json_document, '$.entry[*].resource?(@.code.coding[0].code == $code)'\n                   PASSING 'kniedistorsion' AS \"code\")\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'vkb_ruptur',\n       COUNT(*)\nFROM   stg_json_docs s\nWHERE  s.layer = 'fhir_bundles_all_patients'\nAND    JSON_EXISTS(s.json_document, '$.entry[*].resource?(@.code.coding[0].code == $code)'\n                   PASSING 'vkb_ruptur' AS \"code\");"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 7. Jonas' app report: JSON meets relational and spatial\n\nAt 11:17 Jonas describes what happened in the health app: free text, a few structured answers and his position.\n\n**Question:** What exactly did Jonas report, and where was he?\n\n**How the query answers it:** At 11:17 Jonas reports his symptoms in the health app. The report is a JSON document with free text, structured answers and the location as a GeoJSON point.\n\n- `JSON_TABLE` reads text and answers as columns; `audible_pop` and `instability` come back as `BOOLEAN`.\n- `JSON_VALUE … RETURNING SDO_GEOMETRY` converts the GeoJSON point directly into a geometry (SRID 4326). In SQL Developer the column can be shown on a map; `SDO_UTIL.TO_WKTGEOMETRY` shows the same point as text (well-known text).\n\n**Result:** twist (*Verdrehtrauma*), a \"Plopp\" (audible pop), swelling within about 10 minutes (0.2 h), the knee feels unstable, pain 6 of 10. Location `POINT (11.4129 49.7693)`, on the descent of the loop above Pottenstein. Remember the free text: step 3 searches for similar cases with it."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT jt.report_id,\n       jt.patient_id,\n       jt.created_ts,\n       jt.free_text,\n       jt.mechanism,\n       jt.audible_pop,\n       jt.swelling_onset_hours,\n       jt.instability,\n       jt.pain_nrs,\n       JSON_VALUE(s.json_document, '$.location' RETURNING SDO_GEOMETRY) AS report_location,\n       SDO_UTIL.TO_WKTGEOMETRY(\n         JSON_VALUE(s.json_document, '$.location' RETURNING SDO_GEOMETRY))  AS location_wkt\nFROM   stg_json_docs s,\n       JSON_TABLE(s.json_document, '$'\n         COLUMNS (report_id             VARCHAR2(30)   PATH '$.report_id',\n                  patient_id            VARCHAR2(10)   PATH '$.patient_id',\n                  created_ts            VARCHAR2(30)   PATH '$.created_ts',\n                  free_text             VARCHAR2(4000) PATH '$.free_text',\n                  mechanism             VARCHAR2(30)   PATH '$.structured_answers.mechanism',\n                  audible_pop           BOOLEAN        PATH '$.structured_answers.audible_pop',\n                  swelling_onset_hours  NUMBER         PATH '$.structured_answers.swelling_onset_hours',\n                  instability           BOOLEAN        PATH '$.structured_answers.instability',\n                  pain_nrs              NUMBER         PATH '$.structured_answers.pain_nrs')) jt\nWHERE  s.layer = 'symptom_report_jonas';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 8. Validate the app report with a JSON Schema\n\nAn app sends whatever its developers programmed – sometimes nonsense. Before a report drives a decision, it has to be valid.\n\n**Question:** Is Jonas' report valid, and how would an invalid report be caught?\n\n**How the query answers it:** An app can send anything. A JSON Schema describes what a valid report looks like: required fields, data types, value ranges (pain on the NRS scale from 0 to 10).\n\n- `IS JSON VALIDATE '<schema>'` is a condition: true for valid documents. As a check constraint it rejects invalid reports at insert time.\n- `DBMS_JSON_SCHEMA.VALIDATE_REPORT` returns the reasons as JSON.\n\nThe query tests the original report and a copy changed with `JSON_TRANSFORM` (pain 12, out of range).\n\n**Result:** the original report is valid. The changed copy is invalid, and the report names the reason down to the field: `$.structured_answers.pain_nrs` – *value greater than maximum value, actual: 12, expected: 10* (JZN-00508). The errors before it report the same failure on the levels above (document, `structured_answers`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH reports AS (\n  SELECT 'original'      AS version,\n         s.json_document AS doc\n  FROM   stg_json_docs s\n  WHERE  s.layer = 'symptom_report_jonas'\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'pain_nrs = 12',\n         JSON_TRANSFORM(s.json_document, SET '$.structured_answers.pain_nrs' = 12)\n  FROM   stg_json_docs s\n  WHERE  s.layer = 'symptom_report_jonas'\n)\nSELECT r.version,\n       CASE WHEN r.doc IS JSON VALIDATE '{\"type\":\"object\",\"required\":[\"report_id\",\"patient_id\",\"free_text\",\"structured_answers\"],\"properties\":{\"report_id\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"pattern\":\"^SR-[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{4}$\"},\"patient_id\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"free_text\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"maxLength\":4000},\"structured_answers\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"required\":[\"mechanism\",\"pain_nrs\"],\"properties\":{\"audible_pop\":{\"type\":\"boolean\"},\"instability\":{\"type\":\"boolean\"},\"pain_nrs\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":10}}}}}'\n            THEN 'valid' ELSE 'invalid' END                         AS is_json_validate,\n       JSON_SERIALIZE(DBMS_JSON_SCHEMA.VALIDATE_REPORT(\n                        JSON_SERIALIZE(r.doc),\n                        '{\"type\":\"object\",\"required\":[\"report_id\",\"patient_id\",\"free_text\",\"structured_answers\"],\"properties\":{\"report_id\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"pattern\":\"^SR-[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{4}$\"},\"patient_id\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"free_text\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"maxLength\":4000},\"structured_answers\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"required\":[\"mechanism\",\"pain_nrs\"],\"properties\":{\"audible_pop\":{\"type\":\"boolean\"},\"instability\":{\"type\":\"boolean\"},\"pain_nrs\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":10}}}}}') PRETTY)                         AS validation_report\nFROM   reports r;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 9. From relational to JSON: a duality view of Jonas' care network\n\nThe care network is stored relationally, because the property graph in step 5 needs it that way. Apps, however, want documents.\n\n**Question:** Can we deliver Jonas' care relations as one JSON document without copying data?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The care network (patients, providers, relations) is stored relationally, because the property graph in step 5 needs it that way. A JSON Relational Duality View delivers the same data as one document per patient, without copying anything: the data stays normalized in the tables.\n\nThe view has no `WITH INSERT UPDATE DELETE` annotations, so it is read-only. It is created once with `CREATE OR REPLACE`."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE OR REPLACE JSON RELATIONAL DUALITY VIEW patient_care_dv AS\nSELECT JSON {'_id'           : p.patient_id,\n             'firstName'     : p.first_name,\n             'lastInitial'   : p.last_initial,\n             'birthYear'     : p.birth_year,\n             'homeCity'      : p.home_city,\n             'insurance'     : p.insurance,\n             'careRelations' : [\n               SELECT JSON {'relationId'   : r.relation_id,\n                            'relationType' : r.relation_type,\n                            'since'        : r.since,\n                            'provider'     : (SELECT JSON {'providerId' : pr.provider_id,\n                                                           'name'       : pr.name,\n                                                           'type'       : pr.provider_type,\n                                                           'specialty'  : pr.specialty,\n                                                           'city'       : pr.city}\n                                              FROM   providers pr\n                                              WHERE  pr.provider_id = r.provider_id)}\n               FROM   patient_provider_relations r\n               WHERE  r.patient_id = p.patient_id ]}\nFROM   patients p;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Jonas' care document\n\n**Question:** Which practices are part of Jonas' care network?\n\n**How the query answers it:** **Result:** one document with his general practitioner (*hat_hausarzt*, PR01) and the two practices where he was treated in 2021 (*war_in_behandlung*): the sports medicine practice PR30 and the physiotherapy PR50. Step 5 continues from here with a graph query."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT JSON_SERIALIZE(d.data PRETTY) AS care_document\nFROM   patient_care_dv d\nWHERE  d.data.\"_id\".string() = 'P0001';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Summary\n\n- Documents stay documents: FHIR bundles and app reports are stored as native JSON and read with SQL (dot notation, `JSON_TABLE`, path filters).\n- JSON connects to the other data types: the GeoJSON location becomes an `SDO_GEOMETRY`, the GP reference `PR01` leads into the care network, the free text goes into the text and vector search.\n- Next: **11b** – what happened on the trail?"
    languageId: markdown
